So here is some source diving to you. (read 'arrow' as any projectile 
except magic stones)

The base dissapearing chance is based on the greatest erosion level and 
enchantment level of the arrow. The expression used is '3 + greatest 
erosion - enchantment'. If this is higher than 1 then there is 1 in that 
number chance that the arrow is not destroyed.

If that number is 1 or lower then there is 1 in 4 chance that the arrow 
is destroyed.

So if your arrows are enchanted +2 or higher and are nor damaged (burnt, 
rotted, corroded etc.) then only 1 in 4 is destroyed. (I imagine that 
this is the reason why initial inventory projectiles are usually 
enchanted to +2.)

Then if the arrow is blessed then there is additional chance that the 
arrow is not destroyed and the chance is based on luck, mainly !rnl(4)

So if rnl(4) returns 0 then the arrow is not destroyed. The intended 
functinality of rnlx) is that it returns a value in the range 0..x-1 
with the tendecy that good luck returns more 0's and bad luck more 
x-1's. But rnl(x) doesn't work as intended (C343-51).

